    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d696">

                <pb facs="0627" n="618" sameAs="frus:frus1940v02/pg618"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.0011 European War 1939/6724: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Diplomatic Agent and Consul General at Tangier</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">White</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the
                        Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Tangier</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-11-18T12:00:00+01:00">November 18,
                            1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received November 18—9:19 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>63. To my 61, November 15, 4 p.m., should be added that Weygand still insists
                    against doing anything that might embarrass the Pétain Government.</p>
                <p>De Gaulle is to return shortly from Africa to London. One of the matters to be
                    discussed with him there is the delicate problem of defining his future position
                    in case General Weygand joined the British.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">White</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
